                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            When in doubt, size up. A larger unit reaches the target faster, then cycles off, while an undersized one runs continuously and still never quite gets there. The running cost difference between a 35-pint and a 50-pint machine is small, and the larger one will usually spend fewer hours actually running. The exception is a cold room, where a bigger compressor unit does not fix the frosting problem; there, look at a desiccant model or a unit specifically rated for lower temperatures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Open floor plans matter too. A dehumidifier dries the air it can reach, so a basement chopped into small rooms with closed doors behaves like several separate spaces. Either leave doors open and put the unit centrally, use more than one portable, or move to a ducted whole-basement system that draws from each room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compressor-vs-desiccant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Compressor, Desiccant, or Whole-Basement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compressor dehumidifiers work like a small air conditioner. A fan pulls air across a cold coil, moisture condenses and drips into the bucket, and the air passes back over a warm coil before leaving the unit. They are the most efficient choice in rooms above about 65 degrees, and almost every portable sold in big-box stores is this type. In cooler air the coil frosts, efficiency falls, and some units stop removing water altogether until they defrost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Desiccant dehumidifiers pass air over a wheel coated in a moisture-absorbing material, then heat part of the wheel to release the captured water. Because they do not rely on a cold coil, their performance holds up in cold rooms, down near freezing in some cases. They are lighter and quieter, but they use a heater, so in warm conditions they draw more electricity per pint removed. They make sense for small, cold, unheated spaces, and not for a large, warm, humid basement in August.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-basement units such as the Aprilaire E080 and the Santa Fe line are compressor machines engineered for basement duty. They have larger coils, better defrost logic, sturdier components, and they drain through a line rather than a bucket. Many can be ducted. They cost more up front but tend to remove more water per kilowatt-hour and last longer, which narrows the gap over a decade of use.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best room temperature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Strength</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Weakness</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable compressor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Above 65 F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $200-$350</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheap, efficient in warm air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Frosts in cool rooms, bucket</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable desiccant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 35-65 F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $250-$400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Works in cold air, quiet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low capacity, more power per pint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole-basement compressor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 50-90 F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $1,100-$1,800 plus install</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High capacity, drains itself</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up-front cost</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A quick way to decide: put a cheap thermometer and hygrometer in the basement for a week in summer. If the temperature stays above the mid-60s, a portable compressor unit will do the job. If it sits in the 50s, plan on either a desiccant unit for a small room or a whole-basement machine for a large one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Drainage: The Feature That Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier that stops for a full bucket is not dehumidifying. A 50-pint unit in a wet space can fill a typical bucket in 12 to 24 hours, and unless someone empties it promptly, humidity creeps back up while the machine sits idle. Over a summer, a bucket-only setup tends to deliver far less drying than the rating suggests, simply because of downtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gravity drainage is the simplest fix. Most portables have a hose port; attach a standard garden hose, run it to a floor drain or sump pit, and make sure the hose slopes downhill the whole way. Set the unit on a sturdy stand or a couple of concrete blocks if you need extra fall. Check that the hose does not kink, and keep the end above any standing water in the pit so the line does not siphon or back up.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the nearest drain is higher than the unit, such as a laundry sink or a window, you need a pump. A built-in pump like the one on the GE APER50LZ can push water up several feet and across a room through thin tubing. An external condensate pump also works with any gravity-drain unit and is often the cheaper route if you already own a good dehumidifier. Either way, put the pump on the list of things you look at monthly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-basement units are built around a drain line from the start. Most installers route them to a floor drain or a sump pit, and some include a pump. If you are in a finished basement, plan the route before the machine arrives, because a drain line running across a carpeted floor is the sort of thing that never gets fixed later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where to Put It and How to Set It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put the dehumidifier where the air is wettest and where air can move freely around it. That is usually the center of the open area or near the damp wall, at least 6 to 12 inches from walls and furniture so the intake and exhaust are not blocked. Avoid tucking it into a closet or behind boxes, which turns a 50-pint machine into a small one. If the basement has a sump pit, placing the unit nearby solves drainage and puts it close to one of the wettest spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close the basement windows and the door at the top of the stairs while it runs. An open window in humid weather lets the dehumidifier try to dry the whole neighborhood. Seal obvious leaks around the rim joist and dryer vent, and make sure the dryer itself vents outdoors; a dryer venting into the basement adds gallons of water every load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the target humidity to 50 percent to start. Use a separate hygrometer rather than trusting the unit, because built-in sensors read the air right at the intake and are often a few points off. Once the space holds steady and the musty smell is gone, try 45 percent if you still see condensation on pipes or windows. Going below 40 rarely adds anything except electricity use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the first two weeks to be the hardest. A basement that has been damp for months holds moisture in its concrete, wood, stored boxes, and drywall, and the unit will run almost continuously while it draws that down. After that, run time usually drops substantially. If it never drops, the basement has an ongoing water source the machine cannot outrun, and you need to look at grading, gutters, or wall sealing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What It Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical Energy Star 50-pint portable draws somewhere around 400 to 600 watts while the compressor runs. At the U.S. average electricity rate of roughly 16 to 18 cents per kilowatt-hour, running continuously would cost about $1.60 to $2.60 a day. In practice a properly sized unit in a basement that has already been dried down cycles on and off, and a monthly cost of roughly $15 to $40 in summer is a reasonable expectation, varying with climate and how leaky the space is.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Monthly Running Cost, Summer</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$15-$40</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Energy Star 50-pint, cycling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$50-$70</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Same unit, running nonstop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$20-$45</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Whole-basement unit, cycling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Saved by skipping a 40% setpoint</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Energy Star matters on a machine that runs for months. Certified units must remove a minimum volume of water per kilowatt-hour, and the gap between a certified model and an old, inefficient one can be a meaningful share of the summer bill. If your current dehumidifier predates the 2019 rating change and is not Energy Star, replacing it may pay back over a few seasons on electricity alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest kilowatt-hour is the one you never use. Fixing gutters and downspouts, sealing obvious air leaks, and setting the target at 50 rather than 40 percent all cut run time more than any feature on the spec sheet. A basement wall coating from our <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">basement wall sealer guide</a> can also reduce how much moisture the concrete hands to the air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-basement units cost more to buy but often remove more water per kilowatt-hour than portables, so their running cost is similar or lower for the same amount of drying. Where they win most clearly is in large or very damp spaces, where a portable would run nonstop and still lose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mold-and-air" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Dehumidifiers, Mold, and Air Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold needs moisture, and in most basements the moisture comes from the air or from damp surfaces. Holding relative humidity at or below 50 percent removes the conditions most indoor molds need to grow, which is why a dehumidifier is the first tool for preventing basement mold. It does not kill mold that is already growing, and it does not remove the spores already floating around.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can see mold on walls, joists, or stored items, clean it before you rely on the dehumidifier to keep it away. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-mold-remover/">best mold removers</a> sorts products by whether they kill mold, lift stains, or prevent regrowth, and explains when a patch is too large for a DIY cleanup. Porous materials that are heavily colonized, like carpet pad or wet drywall, usually need to come out.
          </p>

          <p>
            An air purifier is the third piece. A true HEPA unit captures airborne spores and helps with the musty smell, especially in a finished basement used as living space. It works best once the humidity is under control, because spores keep coming as long as something is still growing. Our guide to the <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">best air purifiers for mold</a> covers sizing and filter types.
          </p>

          <p>
            If anyone in the house has asthma, allergies, or a weakened immune system and the basement has visible growth larger than a few square feet, bring in a professional. The EPA suggests that areas larger than about 10 square feet deserve professional advice, and contaminated HVAC ducts should always be handled by someone qualified.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Maintenance That Keeps It Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the air filter every two to four weeks during heavy use. A clogged filter starves the coil of air, cuts extraction, and makes the compressor work harder. Most portable filters rinse clean under a tap; let them dry fully before reinstalling. Whole-basement units use pleated filters that are replaced rather than washed, usually every six to twelve months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wipe out the bucket and check the drain hose monthly. Standing water and warm air are an invitation to slime and bacteria, which can clog a hose or a pump and bring back the very smell you bought the machine to remove. A splash of diluted vinegar in the bucket or a rinse of the hose a few times a season keeps things clear. Never mix cleaning products in a bucket or drain line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the coils once a season. Dust on the coil fins acts like insulation and reduces how much water condenses. A soft brush or a vacuum with a brush attachment is enough; do not bend the fins or use a pressure washer. If you see frost on the coils during normal operation, the room is probably too cold for that unit, or the filter is blocked.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, if you shut the unit off, drain it fully, clean the filter, and store it upright. If you move it, let it stand upright for several hours before switching it on so the compressor oil settles. A dehumidifier that collects no water while the fan runs and the room is humid usually has a refrigerant or compressor problem, which is a warranty call rather than a home repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the old pint rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A listing that says 70 pints may use the pre-2019 test, while a newer 50-pint unit removes about the same amount under the current standard. Comparing the two at face value leads people to overpay for an older model or buy something smaller than they think. Check which test each figure uses before you compare.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the bucket in a wet basement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50-pint machine in a wet space often fills its bucket in less than a day, then sit idle until someone notices. Humidity rebounds during that downtime, and the basement never fully dries. Use a hose to a floor drain, a pump model, or a whole-basement unit with a drain line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a compressor unit in a cold room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Below roughly 60 degrees, the coil of a standard compressor dehumidifier frosts and spends long stretches defrosting. The unit hums along while collecting very little water. Measure the basement temperature first, and use a desiccant or low-temperature whole-basement model if it sits in the 50s.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the humidity too low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dialing the target down to 35 percent does not protect a basement any better than 45 to 50 percent, but it can double run time and electricity use. It also wears out the compressor sooner. Set 50, confirm with a separate hygrometer, and only go lower if condensation persists.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the water source</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier treats damp air; it cannot keep up with water pouring in through a cracked wall or pooling at the foundation after storms. If the unit runs nonstop after the first couple of weeks, check gutters, downspouts, grading, and wall cracks before buying a bigger machine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier does a basement need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most basements up to about 1,500 square feet with ordinary summer dampness, a 50-pint unit on the current DOE test is the right size. Smaller, only mildly musty spaces can use a 35-pint unit. Very large or persistently wet basements, especially those with damp walls, are better served by an 80-pint-class whole-basement machine. When in doubt, size up rather than down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a basement dehumidifier be set to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Set it to 50 percent to start and check the reading with a separate hygrometer. If the musty smell is gone and there is no condensation on pipes or windows, leave it there. Drop to 45 percent if you still see moisture. Going below 40 percent wastes electricity without adding meaningful protection against mold, which struggles to grow below about 50 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a basement dehumidifier run all the time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It should run whenever humidity is above the set point, which in a damp basement can mean nearly continuously for the first week or two. After the space dries out, a correctly sized unit should cycle on and off. If it never stops after a couple of weeks, the unit is undersized, the room is too cold for it, or water is still getting in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier still work in a cold basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard compressor units lose much of their capacity below about 60 to 65 degrees because the coil frosts. Desiccant units keep working in much colder air, and whole-basement compressor units designed for below-grade spaces handle the 50s far better than portables. If your basement stays cool year-round, choose one of those types instead of a typical big-box portable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a built-in pump worth paying for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                When the drain is higher than the dehumidifier or too far away for a downhill hose. A floor drain or sump pit next to the unit works fine with a gravity hose. A laundry sink, a window, or a drain across the room usually needs a pump, either built into the unit or as an external condensate pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier get rid of basement mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will help stop new growth by keeping humidity below the level most molds need, but it will not kill mold that is already there or remove stains. Clean visible growth with an appropriate product, discard heavily contaminated porous materials, and then use the dehumidifier to keep the space dry. Get professional help for patches larger than about 10 square feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a basement dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical Energy Star 50-pint portable uses roughly 400 to 600 watts while running. Cycling normally in a dried-out basement, that often works out to around $15 to $40 a month in summer at average U.S. electricity rates. Running nonstop costs more, which is why fixing air leaks and water sources pays off quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
